/-!
# The linear system of sections vanishing on a closed subscheme

Let `k` be a field, `X` a proper scheme over `k`, `ℒ` an ample invertible `𝒪_X`-module and
`Z ⊂ X` a closed subscheme, presented by its quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals `I`. The *linear
system* `V_n` of [Stacks, Tag 0FD5] is the kernel of the restriction map

`Γ(X, ℒ ^⊗ n) ⟶ Γ(Z, ℒ ^⊗ n|_Z)`,

a `k`-subspace of `Γ(X, ℒ ^⊗ n)`. This file constructs `V_n` and the projective space `P(V_n)`,
proves that for all sufficiently positive `n` the subspace `V_n` generates `ℒ ^⊗ n` on the open
complement `X ∖ Z`, and builds from a basis of `V_n` the morphism `X ∖ Z ⟶ P(V_n)` over `k` that
the generation determines.

The proof is the source's own. The sections of the subsheaf `I ℒ ^⊗ n` of `ℒ ^⊗ n` vanish on `Z`,
so they lie in `V_n`; the ideal sheaf is quasi-coherent and of finite type because `X` is locally
Noetherian, so clause (7) of [Stacks, Tag 01Q3] makes `I ⊗ ℒ ^⊗ n` — and hence its image
`I ℒ ^⊗ n` — generated by finitely many global sections in every sufficiently positive degree;
and off the support of `I` the inclusion `I ℒ ^⊗ n ⟶ ℒ ^⊗ n` is surjective on stalks, because on
an affine open disjoint from the support the subsheaf is everything.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.restrictionMap`: the `k`-linear restriction map
  `Γ(X, F) ⟶ Γ(Z, F|_Z)` for a closed subscheme `Z = V(I)` with its `k`-scheme structure.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.vanishingSections`: **the linear system `V_n`**, the kernel
  of that map at `F = ℒ ^⊗ n`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.projectiveSpaceOfVanishingSections`: **`P(V_n)`**, the
  projective space of the linear system, as a scheme over `Spec k`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.GeneratesOffSupport`: the generation conclusion at one
  degree — the germs of the sections in `V_n` span the stalk of `ℒ ^⊗ n` at every point of
  `X ∖ Z`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.homOfCover`: the glueing step of [Stacks, Tag 01NE] as
  data — the morphism to `ℙᴺ_ℤ` glued from chart families of sections of the structure sheaf that
  span the unit ideal and agree up to a unit on each overlap.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.chartOpen`,
  `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.chartSection`: the charts of an open subscheme `U` on which
  a given global section does not vanish, and the ratios `s_k / sᵢ` read on them.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.toProjectiveSpaceOfVanishingSections`: **the morphism
  `X ∖ Z ⟶ P(V_n)` attached to a basis of `V_n`.**
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.coordIso`: the coordinate isomorphism `ℙᴺ_k ≅ P(V_n)` of
  [Stacks, Tag 0FCY] read as an isomorphism of schemes.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.restrictionMap_app_ιModules_eq_zero`: a global section of
  the subsheaf `I F` of `F` restricts to zero on `Z`. This is the inclusion
  `Γ(X, I ℒ ^⊗ n) ⊆ V_n`, the direction of the source's identification of the two that its proof
  uses.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.surjStalk_ιModules`: off the support of `I` the inclusion
  `I F ⟶ F` is surjective on stalks.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.generatesOffSupport_of_isFinGen`: if `I ℒ ^⊗ n` is
  generated by finitely many global sections then `V_n` generates `ℒ ^⊗ n` off `Z`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.exists_isFinGen_idealPowerMultiple_tensorPowModule`: on a
  locally Noetherian scheme with `ℒ` ample, `I ℒ ^⊗ n` is generated by finitely many global
  sections for all sufficiently positive `n`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.exists_bound_generatesOffSupport`: **the generation
  conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 0FD5]**.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.span_range_basisSection_eq_top`: **a basis of `V_n`
  generates `ℒ ^⊗ n` wherever `V_n` does**, because the germ of a `k`-linear combination is a
  stalk combination of the germs of the basis.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.chartSection_agree`,
  `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.isOpenCover_chartOpen`: the two hypotheses the glueing
  consumes, for the charts of an open subscheme.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.ι_toProjectiveSpaceAtUniverseOffSupport`: **the morphism is
  the one [Stacks, Tag 01NE] describes** — on the chart where `bᵢ` does not vanish it is the
  morphism of the ratios `b_k / bᵢ`.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.toProjectiveSpaceOfVanishingSections_comp_toSpec`: **the
  morphism to `P(V_n)` is a morphism over `k`.**
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.isImmersion_iff_isImmersion_toProjectiveSpaceOver`: whether
  it is an immersion does not depend on the coordinate presentation.
* `Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.exists_bound_hom_projectiveSpaceOfVanishingSections`:
  the morphism in every sufficiently positive degree.

**[Stacks, Tag 0FD5] itself — generation off `Z` together with the canonical morphism of
[Stacks, Tag 0FCY] being an immersion over `k` — is
`Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.exists_bound_generatesOffSupport_and_isImmersion`, in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/VanishingSystemImmersion/Basic.lean`.** Nothing in this file
carries the tag: each declaration below is one clause of it. The morphism the tag speaks about is
the one of [Stacks, Tag 0FCY] applied to the restricted linear system, and
`Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.toProjectiveSpaceOfVanishingSections_eq`, in that file,
proves it to be the morphism glued here.

## Implementation notes

**A closed subscheme is given by its ideal sheaf data.** `Z` is `I.subscheme` for
`I : X.IdealSheafData`, which loses nothing: `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.ker` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.subscheme` are mutually inverse on closed immersions
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_subschemeι`), and the open complement `X ∖ Z` is
`I.support.compl` because `Set.range I.subschemeι = I.support`.

**The subsheaf `I F` is used in place of `I ⊗ F`.** `Stacks.Algebraization.idealPowerMultiple I 1
F` is the subsheaf of `F` generated by the products of sections of `I` with sections of `F`, and
it is the shape in which both inputs are available: the vanishing on `Z` of its sections is
`Stacks.Algebraization.pullback_map_idealPowerMultiple_ι_eq_zero`, and the comparison with the
tensor product is the epimorphism
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealPowerColimit.idealPowTensorToSmul`, which is what carries the
global generation of `I ⊗ ℒ ^⊗ n` over to it. For an invertible `ℒ ^⊗ n` that epimorphism is an
isomorphism, but only surjectivity is needed and only surjectivity is used.

**The spanning condition is stated over the `RingCat` stalk.** The span in
`GeneratesOffSupport` is taken over `TopCat.Presheaf.stalk X.ringCatSheaf.obj x`, the spelling in
which `AlgebraicGeometry.generates_iff_stalkwise_generates` and
`Stacks.AmpleLocalGeneration.IsFinGenOn` test generation, so that
`Stacks.SectionTensorFormula.span_transport` applies to it on the nose. The module structure is
the scoped instance `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.ringStalkModule`, not the `CommRingCat` one of
`Stacks.ModuleStalk.stalkModule`.

**Two spellings of the stalk ring, and the bridge between them.** `GeneratesOffSupport` spans
over the stalk of `X.ringCatSheaf`, while `Stacks.ModuleStalk.maximalSmul` — through which
non-vanishing of a section is defined — is a submodule over `X.presheaf.stalk x`. The two actions
on a fixed stalk element agree, and `Stacks.FinGenFromCover.exists_ringStalk_smul_eq` carries a
scalar one way; `Stacks.StalkwiseGeneration.exists_stalk_smul_eq` carries it back, which is what
lets `Stacks.StalkwiseGeneration.maximalSmulRing` be formed and the chart cover be extracted from
the spanning hypothesis. No isomorphism of the two stalk rings is constructed.

**Generation is by the whole of `V_n`.** The source asserts that `V_n` generates, and that is
what is stated; the proof in fact produces a finite subfamily of `V_n` whose germs already span,
through `Stacks.AmpleLocalGeneration.isFinGenOn_of_isFinGen`, and the statement follows by
monotonicity of the span.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0FD5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FD5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01Q3](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01Q3)
