## What is not done

**The immersion clause of Tag 0FD5 is not proved *here*.** It is proved in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/VanishingSystemImmersion/Basic.lean`, for the canonical morphism of
[Stacks, Tag 0FCY] attached to the linear system restricted to `X ∖ Z`, and the tag attribute sits
there. What this file provides towards it is the generation
(`Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.exists_bound_generatesOffSupport`); what it does *not*
provide is the morphism the tag is about. The glued morphism below is a second construction, kept
for its consumers, and it is not compared with the one of [Stacks, Tag 0FCY]: all that is recorded
of it is that being an immersion is insensitive to the coordinate presentation
(`Stacks.SectionsVanishingOnSubscheme.isImmersion_iff_isImmersion_toProjectiveSpaceOver`).

**The identification of `V_n` with `Γ(X, I ⊗ ℒ ^⊗ n)` is proved in one direction only.** What is
proved is that sections of the subsheaf `I ℒ ^⊗ n` lie in `V_n`, which is the direction the
generation argument needs. The reverse inclusion, that a section vanishing on `Z` is a section of
`I ℒ ^⊗ n`, is the left exactness of global sections applied to
`Stacks.ClosedSubscheme.idealShortComplex` after twisting, and the twisted sequence is not
available: `Stacks.ClosedSubscheme.shortExact_idealShortComplex` is the untwisted sequence
`0 ⟶ I ⟶ 𝒪_X ⟶ ι_* 𝒪_Z ⟶ 0`, and no identification of `F / I F` with `ι_* ι^* F` for a general
`F` is available — only `Stacks.Algebraization.quotientSubschemeComparisonMap`, whose
invertibility is hypothesised rather than proved
(`Stacks.Algebraization.QuotientSubschemeComparison`).

**The morphism depends on a chosen basis of `V_n`, and the source's morphism does not.** Tag
0FD5 speaks of *the* canonical morphism `X ∖ Z ⟶ P(V_n)`, which is basis free. The construction
here fixes a basis of `V_n` indexed by `Fin (N + 1)`, and uses it twice: the basis sections are
what Tag 01NE's construction consumes, and a basis is also what identifies `P(V_n)` with `ℙᴺ_k`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.basisProjectiveSpaceOfModuleIso`, Tag 0FCY). Every declaration below
therefore carries the basis as an argument, and none claims canonicity: that two bases give the
same morphism is **not** proved here. What *is* available, and is what the immersion proof in the
sibling file uses, is the behaviour of Tag 01ND's morphism under a linear change of the
coordinate sections, `Stacks.ProjectiveSpaceChartRatio.toPolynomialModel_comp_map_of_sum` — so
the two morphisms differ by the linear automorphism of `ℙᴺ_k` that the change of basis induces,
and being an immersion is insensitive to the choice. No choice is made inside the construction —
the basis is an argument and not a `Classical.choice` — and the existence of a basis is not
asserted either; see the next note.

**The degree `N + 1` is supplied by the caller.** `V_n` is finite dimensional over `k` because
`X` is proper, but no finite-dimensionality statement for the global sections of a coherent sheaf
on a proper scheme is available in the repository: the declarations carrying Tags 0FD2 and 0FD3,
`AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_globalSections_iff_geometricallyConnected` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_globalSections_tfae_geometricallyConnected_irreducible_integral`, assert
that `k ⟶ Γ(X, 𝒪_X)` is an isomorphism and say nothing about dimensions. So the basis, and with
it the number of homogeneous coordinates, is a hypothesis rather than something produced here.

**Properness is used only through local Noetherianity in this file.** The hypothesis
`IsProper f` enters exactly once below, to supply `IsLocallyNoetherian X`, which is what makes the
ideal sheaf of finite type. (The immersion half, in the sibling file, uses it a second time, for
the finite type hypothesis of [Stacks, Tag 01VS].) The finite-dimensionality of `Γ(X, ℒ ^⊗ n)`
over `k` that properness also gives is not used: `AlgebraicGeometry.projectiveSpaceOfModule` is
defined for an arbitrary module, and the basis of `V_n` the morphism is built from is a hypothesis
rather than a consequence, as the note above records.
-/

